--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
What is vendored, verbatim, from the commit above
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  src/vendor/handlers/   141 files   QuickBooks entity operations
  src/vendor/tools/      141 files   MCP tool definitions and zod schemas
  src/vendor/helpers/      3 files   search criteria, tool registration (see below)
  src/vendor/types/        5 files   ambient module declarations
  src/vendor/LICENSE                 upstream Apache-2.0 licence text

These files are unmodified. Do not edit them. Changes belong in src/runtime/,
so that re-syncing with upstream stays a copy operation rather than a merge.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
What is deliberately NOT vendored, and why
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  src/index.ts
      Binds StdioServerTransport and imports all 141 tools at module load.
      This service speaks Streamable HTTP and exposes a curated allowlist.

  src/auth-server.ts
      Runs an interactive browser OAuth flow. A server must never do this.

  src/helpers/format-error.ts
      Reduces every non-2xx response to "Error: Request failed with status code
      401", discarding the QuickBooks fault body that the axios error carries in
      response.data. That is precisely where the useful signal lives: 401 for an
      expired token, 400 with fault code 5010 for a stale SyncToken, 429 for
      throttling. A replacement occupies the same import path and reads only the
      status and fault fields — never the error's config, which holds the request
      headers and therefore the bearer token.

  src/clients/quickbooks-client.ts
      Reads credentials from process.env at module scope, calls dotenv.config
      with override on a file relative to the installed module, holds a static
      singleton, and can start a browser OAuth flow on port 8000. The result is
      one QuickBooks company per process, which makes multi-tenancy impossible.
      A replacement occupies the same import path (src/vendor/clients/
      quickbooks-client.ts) so the 141 vendored handlers need no edits.

Upstream dependencies dropped: dotenv and open (no filesystem credentials, no
browser), intuit-oauth (token acquisition and refresh belong to the calling API,
not to this service).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Vendored tools that are superseded rather than exposed
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

These vendored tool files still exist under src/vendor/tools/ and are still
byte-identical to upstream, but nothing imports them: the allowlist points at a
replacement in src/runtime/tools/ under the same tool name. OVERRIDDEN_TOOLS in
src/runtime/tool-allowlist.ts is the machine-checked list, and startup fails if
one of these names goes missing.

  update_invoice
      Upstream reads the invoice then posts {...existing, ...patch, sparse:true}.
      Spreading the whole entity defeats a sparse update: every field is present,
      so a concurrent edit made between our read and our write is silently
      reverted. Replaced with a genuinely sparse payload plus one retry on fault
      5010.

  search_invoices, search_customers, search_items, search_terms,
  search_tax_codes
      Two defects. The exposed schema is `criteria: z.any()`, and the runtime
      schema meant to police it is a zod union whose first member,
      z.record(z.any()), matches every object — so the ALLOWED_FILTER_FIELDS
      refinement is unreachable and field names reach the query string
      unvalidated, where node-quickbooks concatenates them without quoting.
      Separately, `fetchAll: true` is accepted, and module.query honours it by
      paging until the company is exhausted and concatenating every record into
      one array. The replacements enforce closed field enums, cap the page at 100
      rows and a byte budget, over-fetch exactly one row to report has_more
      exactly, and cannot express fetchAll at all.

  get_customer, read_item, get_company_info
      Uncached, and reads are the metered half of Intuit's pricing. The
      replacements cache per realm with an explicit TTL and entry ceiling.
      get_company_info additionally dropped its caller-supplied company_id: the
      company is whichever one the request's grant is for.

  get_invoice_pdf
      Returns the document base64-encoded inline, or writes it to the filesystem
      when an environment variable allows it. The replacement fetches once, holds
      the bytes under a single-use 256-bit handle bound to the realm, and returns
      a link redeemable through GET /v1/pdf/{realmId}/{handle}.

  delete_invoice
      Not exposed at all, and not replaced under the same name: cancellation is
      void_invoice. See the re-sync notes below.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The multi-tenancy seam
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

All 141 vendored handlers reach QuickBooks through exactly two static methods:

  QuickbooksClient.getInstance()          used by all 141
  QuickbooksClient.getAuthCredentials()   used by create-quickbooks-attachable

Both are re-implemented to resolve the current request's tenant (realm id and
Intuit access token) from an AsyncLocalStorage context. Every handler therefore
becomes tenant-aware without a single edit to vendored code, and a call made
outside a tenant scope fails closed instead of falling back to ambient
credentials.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Re-syncing with upstream
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  1. Pick the target upstream commit and record its SHA here.
  2. Download that commit's tarball.
  3. Replace src/vendor/{handlers,tools,helpers,types} and src/vendor/LICENSE
     wholesale. Never merge into them.
  4. Do not copy index.ts, auth-server.ts, clients/quickbooks-client.ts or
     helpers/format-error.ts — all four are deliberately replaced (see above).
  5. Run the type check. Breakage appears as changed call signatures against
     src/runtime/, which is the intended blast radius.
  6. Re-read the diff for new tools: newly added tools are not exposed until
     they are added to the allowlist in src/runtime/.
  7. Re-check upstream's delete and write handlers for behaviour changes before
     exposing anything new. As of the pinned commit, delete-quickbooks-invoice
     attempts a genuine hard delete before falling back to a void, which is why
     it is not on the allowlist.
  8. Re-check the superseded tools listed above. If upstream has fixed one of
     them, the replacement can be retired — but verify the fix rather than
     assuming it, and remove the name from OVERRIDDEN_TOOLS in the same change so
     the startup check keeps matching reality.
